Date: Thu, 8 Jul 93 23:49:21 EDT
From: eisrael@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: EGO: Alfalfa Males and Other Bean Sprouts

I'm writing to share my amusement (and frustration) with recent exchanges
about neopagans, alpha males, hyper-rationality and so on.


ALPHA MALES
I'm amused no end by the finger-pointing and name calling on the "alpha
male" front. Let's review: This mailing list is for extropians, who
believe that the universe, its resources, and the arena of human
action are literally *without bound*.  Extropians are people who've
taken up the first lie of the Father of Lies as a rallying cry: "Thou
shalt not surely die!" Let's face it: Extropianism has gone several
miles past Ambition right into the heart of Downtown Hubris.  And we
celebrate it!

And then we have the unmitigated gall to point fingers at one another
and accuse each other of *egotism*? That's like accusing a black hole of
being dense. So Eric's pretty impressed with himself? So what? So's
Perry, and Ray, and all of the rest of us. (Myself included. Why else do
you suppose I so love to hear myself talk?)


HYPER-RATIONALITY
As long as I've been reading this list, I don't think you could find a
more heated argument during which *none of the participants could offer
any specific points of disagreement with the other side.* The entire
"debate" has consisted of posts by both sides that disclaim the
positions with which they claim to have been erroneously associated by
their opponants. One wonders whether the parties are debating each
other, or merely reliving old debates in each other's presence.

That rationality is not always useful or appropriate is almost
tautological. It's also a very *rational* statement. After all, what's
so rational about me waving my arms furiously and prancing around my
living room while listening to Beethoven? Not a damned thing, but
I enjoyed it immensely anyway and spent a significant fraction of my
evening doing it. And I hardly count as one o' them "long-haired leaping
gnome" types.


NEOPAGANS
You know, in polite company it used to be the practice to avoid
discussions of a personal nature, particularly of topics that were apt
to cause acrimonious disagreement and which were unlikely to reach any
kind of mutually satisfactory conclusion, like, say, religion. I thought
that one of the purposes of a mailing list, as opposed to a usenet news
group, was to engender polite discussion.

Apparently, however, we're supposed to judge each other's claims to
extropian correctness and mental fitness like some kind of Ayn Rand
sewing circle. Or perhaps there's just too much ego (and testosterone)
floating around for us to admit when we've had a bad idea and back away
from it.

				* * *

I'm sorry. This note has come out somewhat more sharply edged than I
intended. But I'm frustrated to see so many of my favorite contributors --
the same people who so often call for and help provide a better S/N
ratio -- degrade the discussion so much with simple bickering.

OK, now that I've offended everyone, who'll bring me up on charges?

Elias Israel
eisrael@east.sun.com

