From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: My One Message

Mike Price:
>> I believe the economy provides a better
>> model for the path to nanotech than analogies with bacteria.  General
>> self-replicating assemblers are a poor, inefficient way to produce
>> miracles.  The industrial revolution is a better miracle to examine. 

Derek Zahn writes:
> Disagreement.  If self-rep nanotech (SRN) possible, represents 
> technological advancement in production.  Precision of interchangeable
> parts made assembly line possible -- big win.  Precision of SRN could
> do it again.
> History of production technology not over.

_Loved_ clipped style.  Like Rorschach from _Watchmen_.  Concise.  But
you haven't shown anything.  All you've done is state that SRN will lead
to great productivity, which is what I disagree with.  Nanotech plus AI
will lead to great productivity, but self-replication is irrelevant. 
Specialisation of function/division of labour strongly implies that the
reproductive and manufacturing functions should be separated.  Consider
the ant.  They are very successful (_huge_ biomass) because they
separated out the reproductive unit (the queen) from their non-defence
units (the workers) and their military (guards).

Self-replication is best left at the level of flows of capital in the
economy.  Capital investment targets the most profitable ventures,
production techniques using the tried and trusted techniques of the
market.  More flexible than hordes of general assemblers fighting in a
vat.  IMO Drexler has virtually ignored the problem of how the hell the
activities of these wee little beasties are co-ordinated.  He mutters
something about a computer program being distributed amongst the
trillions of GAs in a vat - he has not considered the organisational
problems involved.  Either each GA is pretty dumb (which is how he
envisages it, I think) in which case _each_ GA requires a _different_
action plan, detailed (of course) down to the molecular level.  Or
they're pretty smart, in which case they start fighting for resources
amongst themselves and you have the classic problems of project
management (an area I _do_ have specific expertise in).

> derek

