Date: Fri, 9 Jul 93 9:53:54 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: General Assemblers (was: My One Message)

Mike Price quite rightly notes that my defense of self-replication
as an advancement of production technology contained no convincing
argument.  Now that the ration coupons are a thing of the past (and
I do hope somebody is writing an epic poem history of the list...
abbreviated witch trial results released in the midst of Black
Thursday, quite dramatic, no?) [but no doubt coded into the new
software against future need], I can do so.

I kind of hope that Mike is right -- the primary intuition behind
self-rep as advancement is elegance.  A more complex, extended,
system might be more pleasing.

> Specialisation of function/division of labour strongly implies that the
> reproductive and manufacturing functions should be separated.  Consider
> the ant.


Why?  Consider the polyp colonies that make up coral reefs.  Why is
the ant a better metaphor than the vast majority of biological forms?

It seems clear that some sort of self-replication needs to take place
to build production capacity of nanodevices.  You suggest that it is
best done at the highest possible level.  I'll argue that instead, if
GAs are possible, they offer a potential for increased efficiency
because the effort needed to effect a localized increase in production
capacity involves only short-range transportation of matter.  The
larger the production infrastructure, the further semi-assembled material
has to be moved around, at great cost in failure modes and energy use.

Now, self-rep GAs (or small groups of them "co-reproducing" or any one
of lots of alternative scenarios [apologies for posting material with
sexual overtones]) have their own efficiency penalties -- all based on
whether they can be effective at both assembling product and other GAs
effectively.  Because the structure of the devices they will construct
will likely be very similar to themselves (little computers, diamondoid
stuff, etc), my intiution tells me that if these kind of GAs can work,
they would be a very effective (and even better -- very flexible!)
production technology.

Though note that Ralph Merkle and lately Drexler agrees with you,
for the most part.  See _Unbounding the Future_, pp 140-141.

> IMO Drexler has virtually ignored the problem of how the hell the
> activities of these wee little beasties are co-ordinated.

This is a very good point; perhaps simple Dynamic Optimism isn't
sufficient here.  Hmm.  I have a vague memory of JoSH or somebody
playing around with the idea of a "utility fog".  Was there any
detail there about how coordination was to take place?

It might be possible to have the "wee little beasties" behave
similar to coral; that is, always be in contact with one another.

If we can have equipment around the vat, it might be possible to
communicate position and command electromagnetically from outside
with phased-array antennas, but I don't know if the resolution
would be good enough (pity if not, because a "matter transmitter"
could work this way).

Has anybody seen proposals for the way assemblers coordinate?
I'd look in Engines of Creation for ideas of what he had in
mind, but I loaned it out and it was never returned.

derek


